---
import Gallery from '../../layouts/Gallery.astro';
import Related from '../../components/Related.astro';
import RfDevice from '../../components/RfDevice.astro';
import rfProfile from '../../data/rf-profile.json';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---
<Gallery title="Pattern: RF task queue" description="What is next for one task type on a handheld: read top to bottom, tap the task at the top, and go.">
  <p class="demo-note">
    <strong>Who uses it</strong>: a warehouse floor worker after picking a
    task from the <a href={base + '/patterns/rf-landing'}>RF task menu</a>
    — "what's next" for that task type. <strong>What "done" looks like</strong>:
    the worker reads top-to-bottom, taps the task at the top, and starts
    working it — no cross-referencing between columns. The task is a plain
    link and the line box makes it a 128&times;42px target at spacious
    density, so it takes a gloved thumb without a button's padding pushing
    the Status column off a 320px screen (measured, roadmap 135.3b). <strong>Not for</strong> editing a
    line's data on this screen — see <a href={base + '/patterns/rf-list#editable-refused'}>Editable, refused</a>
    below. Composed from <code>.bo-data-table</code> at
    <code>data-density="spacious"</code>, two narrow columns — the SAME
    component and profile membership <a href={base + '/patterns/goods-receipt'}>Goods
    receipt</a>'s own receiving log already proves at 360px (roadmap 59.4);
    this page is a second, task-queue-shaped use of that same proof, not a
    new claim about the component.
  </p>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>The screen — try it</h2>
    <p>Shown the way the scanner gets it: the <code>rf-essentials</code>
    profile (Chrome/WebView&nbsp;108) only, at 360×640, in an isolated
    document. No profile change was needed for this page:
    <code>data-table</code> was already a member. One screen, not two — this
    page rendered the same component a second time at desktop width until
    roadmap 135.2.</p>
    <ul class="bo-cluster docs-list-bare">
      {rfProfile.components.map((c) => <li><a href={base + c.href}><code>{c.name}</code></a></li>)}
    </ul>
    <RfDevice src={base + '/patterns/rf/rf-list-rf/'} title="RF scanner, rf-essentials profile at 360×640 — starts on the task queue, and its links walk the whole track" />
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Markup</h2>
    <pre><code>{`<div class="bo-data-table-container" tabindex="0" data-density="spacious">
  <table class="bo-data-table">
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Task</th><th scope="col">Status</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>PO-88213 · Dock 4</td><td><span class="bo-badge bo-badge--accent">Next</span></td></tr>
      <!-- one row per queued task -->
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>`}</code></pre>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Anatomy</h2>
    <ol class="bo-stack bo-stack--tight docs-list">
      <li><strong>Queue table</strong> — <code>.bo-data-table</code> at
        spacious density, two columns: identifying label, status.</li>
      <li><strong>Next-up row</strong> — the top row carries a distinct
        <code>.bo-badge--accent</code> status, the row a worker taps first.</li>
      <li><strong>Row tap target</strong> <span class="bo-u-text-muted">(not in this demo — a real screen wraps each row in a link/button so the whole row is tappable, not just the text)</span>.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Data contract</h2>
    <div class="bo-data-table-container" tabindex="0">
      <table class="bo-data-table" data-density="compact">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Request</th><th scope="col">Returns / on failure</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="bo-data-table__col--code">GET /rf/tasks/:type</td><td>the ordered queue, next-up first; 204 for an empty queue</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="bo-data-table__col--code">POST /rf/tasks/:id/claim</td><td>marks the task in-progress and returns the detail screen route; 409 if another worker already claimed it — re-render the queue, don't just retry</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>States</h2>
    <div class="bo-data-table-container" tabindex="0">
      <table class="bo-data-table" data-density="compact">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">State</th><th scope="col">What ships</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Loading</td><td>a skeleton row set at the same spacious row height, so the tap targets don't jump once data lands</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Empty — nothing queued</td><td>"All caught up" text plus a link back to the <a href={base + '/patterns/rf-landing'}>task menu</a>, not a bare blank table</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Empty — filtered out</td><td>N/A on this screen — the queue has no filter control; filtering happens by picking a different task type from the menu</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Claim conflict (409)</td><td>the row is removed from this worker's queue with a brief alert; no destructive retry</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Session lost</td><td>the reconnect state: the queue re-renders as the server has it, and a claim attempted while the link was down is simply retried — claiming is idempotent server-side (the 409 row above). Offline caching is infrastructure, not a screen promise</td></tr>
          <tr><td>No JS</td><td>fully functional — this page has no JS dependency; the demo has no button or script wired to it at all, and it imports no <code>@busy-office/ui/js</code> behavior</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="demo" id="editable-refused">
    <h2>Editable, refused</h2>
    <p>An editable variant of this list — letting a worker change quantity or
    status inline in the grid — was grilled and <strong>refused</strong>
    (roadmap 109.7): RF data correction is a one-field-at-a-time <strong>scan
    flow</strong> (barcode, then quantity, then confirm — see
    <a href={base + '/patterns/goods-receipt'}>Goods receipt</a>), not grid
    editing with gloves on a 360px screen. A generic
    <a href={base + '/patterns/editable-grid'}>editable grid</a> composes for
    desktop entry; it does not compose for RF, and forcing it to would be
    the RF track absorbing the app catalogue's shape instead of its own
    (109.6's founding rule).</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Components used</h2>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted"><span class="bo-badge">complexity 1 of 4</span></p>
    <ul class="bo-cluster docs-list-bare">
      <li><a class="bo-badge bo-badge--type" href={base + '/components/data-table'}>Data table</a></li>
      <li><a class="bo-badge bo-badge--type" href={base + '/components/badge'}>Badge</a></li>
      <li><a class="bo-badge bo-badge--type" href={base + '/concepts/density'}>Density (spacious)</a></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <Related links={[
    ["/patterns/rf-landing", "RF task menu"],
    ["/patterns/goods-receipt", "RF goods receipt"],
    ["/patterns/editable-grid", "Editable grid (desktop)"],
  ]} />
</Gallery>
